# Configure ReqLLM

This tutorial shows what Jido AI hands to ReqLLM: the model name, the provider key, and the options on each call. ReqLLM is the default model layer. Every cell runs against a local client that records its arguments, so you can read the exact call without a provider. The one provider fence is marked and is not for this notebook.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "migration/consolidated-v3"}
])
```

## Provide a provider key

`Jido.AI.InferenceClient.ReqLLM` is the default client. It passes each call to the matching `ReqLLM` function, and ReqLLM finds the provider key. Jido AI has no key setting of its own. In your application, set an environment variable such as `OPENAI_API_KEY` or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, or use the ReqLLM config:

<!-- partial -->
```elixir
# config/runtime.exs
config :req_llm,
  openai_api_key: System.fetch_env!("OPENAI_API_KEY"),
  receive_timeout: 120_000
```

Under Mix, ReqLLM also loads a `.env` file from the current directory at startup. Set `config :req_llm, load_dotenv: false` to turn that off. Read the ReqLLM configuration guide for pools, timeouts, and provider base URLs.

Keep keys out of Signal data and reasoning defaults. A request's `options` are ordinary Flow input.

## Name a model

A model name is a `provider:model` string. ReqLLM resolves it from its bundled catalog without a network call.

```elixir
model = ReqLLM.model!("anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5")

{model.provider, String.starts_with?(model.id, "claude-haiku-4-5")}
```

The cell returns `{:anthropic, true}`. The catalog resolves this short name to a dated release, so `model.id` can be longer than the name you wrote. Use exact, versioned names when a provider publishes them, so that a model change is your choice. A reasoning Flow's `model` field accepts strings only.

## Record what the client receives

This client stores each call in an `Agent` and returns a fixed answer.

```elixir
defmodule ReqLLMGuide.Recorder do
  @behaviour Jido.AI.InferenceClient

  def start, do: Agent.start_link(fn -> nil end, name: __MODULE__)
  def last_call, do: Agent.get(__MODULE__, & &1)

  @impl true
  def generate_text(model, context, options) do
    Agent.update(__MODULE__, fn _previous -> %{model: model, options: options} end)

    {:ok,
     %ReqLLM.Response{
       id: "local",
       model: model,
       context: context,
       message: ReqLLM.Context.assistant(~s({"priority":"high"})),
       object: if(options[:output], do: %{"priority" => "high"}),
       finish_reason: :stop,
       usage: %{input_tokens: 4, output_tokens: 3}
     }}
  end

  @impl true
  def evaluation_models, do: []

  for {name, arity} <- Jido.AI.InferenceClient.behaviour_info(:callbacks),
      {name, arity} not in [generate_text: 3, evaluation_models: 0] do
    @impl true
    def unquote(name)(unquote_splicing(Macro.generate_arguments(arity, __MODULE__))),
      do: {:error, :not_supported}
  end
end

defmodule ReqLLMGuide.TriageAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, name: "triage_agent"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      model "anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5"
      options %{temperature: 0.0, max_tokens: 200, receive_timeout: 20_000}

      limits do
        elapsed_ms 30_000
      end
    end
  end
end

{:ok, _recorder} = ReqLLMGuide.Recorder.start()

run_request = fn params ->
  agent = ReqLLMGuide.TriageAgent.instantiate!(id: "triage-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")

  {:ok, _agent, [%{signal: reply}]} =
    ReqLLMGuide.TriageAgent.cmd(
      agent,
      ReqLLMGuide.TriageAgent.request_signal!(params),
      context: %{jido_ai_inference_client: ReqLLMGuide.Recorder}
    )

  reply.data
end

%{status: :completed} = run_request.(%{input: "Triage ticket T-12."})

ReqLLMGuide.Recorder.last_call().options |> Keyword.keys() |> Enum.sort()
```

The cell returns `[:max_tokens, :receive_timeout, :telemetry, :temperature, :total_timeout]`. The first, second, and fourth come from `options`. Jido adds the others, as the next section shows.

## Know what Jido adds

`Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.CallModel` builds the final option list from `options`:

| Option | Source |
| --- | --- |
| Anything in `options` | Passed through as a keyword list. |
| `:tool_choice` | The `tool_policy` requirement. A value set in `options` is removed. |
| `:tools` | The selected tools, when there are any. |
| `:total_timeout` | The smaller of any `total_timeout` in `options` and the time left in `limits.elapsed_ms`. |
| `:telemetry` | Your map with `conversation_id` set to the Thread ID. |
| `:output`, `:output_validation` | Set from `output_schema`, with `:strict` validation. |
| `:provider_options` | Prompt cache settings, when enabled. |

```elixir
options = ReqLLMGuide.Recorder.last_call().options

%{
  timeout_within_budget: options[:total_timeout] <= 30_000,
  telemetry_keys: Map.keys(options[:telemetry])
}
```

The cell returns `%{timeout_within_budget: true, telemetry_keys: [:conversation_id]}`.

## Request structured output

Set `output_schema` to a JSON Schema map. ReqLLM receives it as an object output. The client must return the decoded object in the response's `:object` field, and it becomes the reply's `output`.

```elixir
schema = %{
  "type" => "object",
  "properties" => %{"priority" => %{"type" => "string"}},
  "required" => ["priority"],
  "additionalProperties" => false
}

structured = run_request.(%{input: "Triage ticket T-12.", output_schema: schema})
options = ReqLLMGuide.Recorder.last_call().options

{structured.output, options[:output_validation], options[:output].type}
```

The cell returns `{%{"priority" => "high"}, :strict, :object}`.

## Turn on prompt caching

`prompt_cache` lowers to provider options by the provider part of the model name. Jido sets options for `openai`, `anthropic`, `amazon_bedrock`, and `bedrock`. Other providers get no cache options, and the request still runs.

| Provider | Options set |
| --- | --- |
| `openai` | `prompt_cache_key` set to the Session ID, and `prompt_cache_options` with `ttl` when set |
| `anthropic` | `anthropic_prompt_cache: true`, and `anthropic_prompt_cache_ttl` when set |
| `amazon_bedrock`, `bedrock` | `prompt_cache: true`, and `prompt_cache_ttl` when set |

```elixir
%{status: :completed} =
  run_request.(%{input: "Triage ticket T-12.", prompt_cache: %{enabled: true, ttl: "1h"}})

anthropic = ReqLLMGuide.Recorder.last_call().options[:provider_options]

%{status: :completed} =
  run_request.(%{
    input: "Triage ticket T-12.",
    model: "openai:gpt-4o-mini",
    prompt_cache: %{enabled: true}
  })

openai = ReqLLMGuide.Recorder.last_call().options[:provider_options]

{anthropic, Map.keys(openai)}
```

The cell returns `{%{anthropic_prompt_cache: true, anthropic_prompt_cache_ttl: "1h"}, [:prompt_cache_key]}`. Each attempt records what the provider reported in its `prompt_cache` field. See [Model Budgets and Usage](budgets-and-usage.md).

## Run against a real provider

Run this in your own application with a key set. It makes a paid call, so it is not part of this notebook's flow. `MyApp.SupportAgent` is your Agent.

<!-- partial -->
```elixir
{:ok, _instance} = Jido.start(name: MyApp.Jido, namespace: "my-app")
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(MyApp.Jido, MyApp.SupportAgent, id: "support-1")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(server)

request =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Summarize the refund policy.",
    model: "openai:gpt-4o-mini",
    options: %{max_tokens: 256, receive_timeout: 60_000}
  })

{:ok, _agent, [%{signal: reply}]} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(server, request, timeout: :infinity, admission_timeout: :infinity)

reply.data.output
```

`call/3` has a 5-second default timeout, which is too short for most model calls. The Agent's `limits.elapsed_ms` still bounds the model calls.

